# Meltdown — Audio

The game plays ten cues, one per event. This file defines the cues and what mute
does.

## The cues

| Cue | The event it answers |
| --- | --- |
| `fire` | An emitter's shot resolves. |
| `trip` | An emitter trips. |
| `death` | A surge unit's hp reaches `0`. |
| `leak` | A surge unit reaches its assigned exhaust. |
| `place` | A tower is placed on the floor. |
| `sell` | A placed tower is sold. |
| `wave-clear` | A wave clears. |
| `victory` | The victory screen opens. |
| `game-over` | The game-over screen opens. |
| `menu` | A menu highlight moves. |

Each cue is short and distinct from the other nine.

A cue is raised by the frame that resolves the event it answers, and it is played
from the frame loop, so a cue always names one real frame: the frame the shot
landed on, the frame the wave cleared on, the frame the tower was placed on. An
event that does not resolve raises nothing, so a placement refused as invalid
plays no `place` cue.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## Playing them

Audio is synthesized with the Web Audio API. The build ships no audio file and
loads none.

Browsers block audio until the page has been interacted with, so the game makes
no sound at all before the first input reaches it: until then it starts no audio
source and plays no clip, whatever a source would have sounded like. It starts
and runs correctly whether or not audio can start.

## Mute

The build owns the mute bit, so the build is what silences the game. It is
toggled by the `mute` action and by the panel's mute control, from any screen,
and `specs/hud.md` states how the control reads.

While muted, none of the ten cues produces any sound: the build plays no muted
cue at all, starting no audio source and playing no clip for it. The game stays
fully playable muted: nothing about the floor, the run, or the screens changes
with the mute bit, so every event that would have carried a cue still resolves,
and every figure it moves still moves.
{{else}}
## Playing them

The cues are defined once, when the game is initialized, and played through the
{{engine.name}} engine's audio bus, which the engine documents in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository. Each cue is synthesized rather than
loaded: the build ships no audio file.

The engine owns the first-interaction unlock. The game plays none of the ten cues
before the first input reaches it and asks the bus for nothing until then,
whatever the bus would have done with the request. It starts and runs correctly
whether or not audio can start.

## Mute

The engine owns the mute bit, so the engine is what silences the game. It is
toggled by the `mute` action and by the panel's mute control, from any screen,
and `specs/hud.md` states how the control reads.

While muted, none of the ten cues produces any sound. The game does not gate its
own cues on the bit: the same events ask the bus for the same cues muted as
unmuted, and the engine plays each of them at an amplitude of zero. The game
stays fully playable muted: nothing about the floor, the run, or the screens
changes with the mute bit, so every event that carries a cue still resolves.
{{/if}}
